%I A399895 #59 Oct 27 2026 21:06:10
%S A399895 1,1,3,3,5,1,5,7,9,9,5,7,11,13,9,9,11,13,17,13,15,15,17,13,17,19,21,
%T A399895 15,17,19,23,19,21,21,23,19,23,25,27,27,23,25,23,31,27,33,23,31,29,37,
%U A399895 27,33,29,37,35,37,33,33,35,37,35,37,39,39,35,37,35,43,39,45
%N A399895 Parity-perturbed Hofstadter-type sequence with delays 3 and 6 and initial values (1,1,3,3,5,1).
%C A399895 The sequence is well-defined for all n >= 1, and 1 <= a(n) <= n. Every term is odd. Every positive odd integer occurs.
%C A399895 For all n >= 2, a(n+6)-a(n) in {0,6}.
%C A399895 The sequence exhibits dyadic oscillations, similar to those of the sequence A394051.
%C A399895 Let s(k) = 2^k - floor((k-1)/2), for k >= 1. Then a(6*s(k)) = 3*2^k + 1.
%C A399895 For the binary words W_k defined in Marco Mantovanelli's "A New Perturbed Hofstadter Q-Recursion: Exact Frequencies and Gaussian Profiles" Section 2, let P_k and V_k count peaks (01) and valleys (10), and let p(k,j) and v(k,j) denote their j-th heights from the left, measured as zeros minus ones in the preceding prefix.
%H A399895 Marco Mantovanelli, <a href="/A399895/b399895.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399895 Benoit Cloitre, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06237">The Mantovanelli-Hofstadter Sequence</a>, arXiv:2604.06237 [math.NT], 2026; see Definition 2.3, Lemmas 2.4-2.5 and 3.3, and Section 4.1 for the interleaving framework.
%H A399895 Marco Mantovanelli, <a href="https://doi.org/10.13140/RG.2.2.36783.70561">A New Perturbed Hofstadter Q-Recursion: Exact Frequencies and Gaussian Profiles</a>, ResearchGate, 2026.
%F A399895 a(1)=a(2)=1, a(3)=a(4)=3, a(5)=5, a(6)=1; for n >= 7, a(n) = a(n-a(n-3)) + a(n-a(n-6)) - (-1)^n.
%F A399895 a(n)/n = 1/2 + O(1/sqrt(log(n))).
%F A399895 For k>=2, P_k=2^(k-2)+(1+(-1)^k)/2=A052531(k-2), V_k=P_k-1=A051049(k-2), P_k+V_k=2^(k-1)+(-1)^k=A062510(k-1), and (P_(k+1)-P_k)/3=A001045(k-2). See Section 5.1, Proposition 5.4, Eqs. (5.9) and (5.12), and Table 1.
%F A399895 For n>=0, P_(2n+2)=4^n+1=A052539(n), P_(2n+3)=2*4^n=A004171(n), V_(2n+2)=4^n=A000302(n), and V_(2n+3)=2*4^n-1=A083420(n). See Section 5.1, Proposition 5.4, Eqs. (5.10)-(5.11), and Table 1.
%F A399895 For j>=1, p(2j,j)=(j-1)^2+1=A002522(j-1), v(2j,j)=(j-1)^2=A000290(j-1), p(2j+1,j)=j*(j-1)+2=A014206(j-1), and v(2j+1,j)=j*(j-1)=A002378(j-1). See Section 5.1, Proposition 5.4, Eqs. (5.13)-(5.14), and Table 1.
%F A399895 The maximum word heights M_k satisfy M_k ~ 2^k/sqrt(2Pik), equivalently 2*M_k/A001405(k) -> 1 as k -> infinity. See Section 7, Theorem 7.1 and Eq. (7.6). This comparison is asymptotic.
%e A399895 a(7) = a(7-a(4)) + a(7-a(1)) - (-1)^7 = a(4) + a(6) + 1 = 3 + 1 + 1 = 5.
%e A399895 a(8) = a(8-a(5)) + a(8-a(2)) - (-1)^8 = a(3) + a(7) - 1 = 3 + 5 - 1 = 7.
%t A399895 a[1] = a[2] = 1;
%t A399895 a[3] = a[4] = 3;
%t A399895 a[5] = 5;
%t A399895 a[6] = 1;
%t A399895 a[n_Integer /; n >= 7] := a[n] =
%t A399895   a[n - a[n - 3]] + a[n - a[n - 6]] - (-1)^n;
%t A399895 Table[a[n], {n, 1, 70}]
%Y A399895 Cf. A394051 (original parity-perturbed Hofstadter Q-sequence), A005185 (Hofstadter Q-sequence).
%Y A399895 Associated word lengths and exact local-extremum statistics: A000079, A052531, A051049, A062510, A001045, A052539, A004171, A000302, A083420, A002522, A000290, A014206, A002378.
%Y A399895 Asymptotic comparison for maximum word heights: A001405.
%K A399895 nonn,easy,new
%O A399895 1,3
%A A399895 _Marco Mantovanelli_, Oct 16 2026
